// =============================================================================
// ENNEAD — a Byzantine-Fault-Tolerant consensus kernel, written in Anubis
// =============================================================================
// In the myth, the Ennead is the tribunal of nine gods of Heliopolis. When Horus
// and Set brought their contendings before it, the council had to reach ONE
// binding verdict even though one of their number — Set, the deceiver — would lie,
// equivocate, and split the room to get his way. That is Byzantine consensus, four
// thousand years early: N judges, some honest, at least one a liar, and a protocol
// that must still agree on a single truth.
//
// ENNEAD is that tribunal as running software. It is a deterministic, offline
// State-Machine-Replication kernel — the beating heart of every blockchain,
// replicated database, sovereign ledger, and fault-tolerant control plane — built
// so it demonstrates BOTH halves of Anubis at once:
//
//   • anubis check examples/industry/ennead_consensus_kernel.anb
//        asks Z3 to PROVE the six integer-safety theorems that make BFT sound —
//        the quorum-intersection lemma among them. If any proof fails, the
//        artifact is REJECTED. The safety of the protocol is not tested; it is
//        proved.
//
//   • anubis run   examples/industry/ennead_consensus_kernel.anb
//        boots a live council of replicas and drives it through five scenarios:
//        the happy path, a crashed leader, an EQUIVOCATION ATTACK (Set himself),
//        a too-many-faults case that must safely STALL rather than split-brain,
//        and a nine-seat council under compound faults. Then it proves the honest
//        replicas' logs are byte-identical (Agreement) and seals them into a
//        tamper-evident hash chain.
//
//   • anubis build examples/industry/ennead_consensus_kernel.anb --evidence
//        seals source + IR + solver query + native artifact into a Proof-Carrying
//        Artifact you can verify and Ed25519-sign.
//
// Language surface exercised: enums (protocol ISA + fault model + verdicts),
// exhaustive `match` with guards, traits with default methods, generics, closures
// with map/filter/reduce/count/any/all, methods on structs, Option/Result + `?`,
// maps for vote tallies, string interpolation, a hash-chained ledger — AND six
// SMT-verified contracts with a loop invariant.
//
//   n = 3f + 1 replicas tolerate f Byzantine faults; a quorum is 2f + 1.
// =============================================================================


// =============================================================================
// PART 1 — THE VERIFIED SAFETY KERNEL  (proved by Z3 at `anubis check` time)
// =============================================================================
// These six functions are the safety theorem of Byzantine consensus, written as
// integer arithmetic the solver can discharge. At runtime they simply compute;
// at check-time each carries a machine-checked proof. Every bound is load-bearing
// (a `u32` annotation is runtime-inert, so the solver assumes nothing about range
// unless a `requires` states it).

// (1) A quorum strictly exceeds the fault budget. With n = 3f+1 a quorum is 2f+1,
//     and 2f+1 > f for every f >= 0 — so a quorum can never be composed entirely
//     of faulty replicas. This is why one honest voice always survives a quorum.
fn quorum_exceeds_faults(f: u32) -> u32
    requires(f >= 0) requires(f < 1000000)
    ensures(result > f)
{
    return 2 * f + 1;
}

// (2) THE QUORUM-INTERSECTION LEMMA — the crown jewel of BFT safety.
//     Any two quorums drawn from n = 3f+1 replicas overlap in at least
//     |A| + |B| - n = 2(2f+1) - (3f+1) = f+1 replicas. Z3 proves the overlap is
//     >= f+1 from the definitions alone. Two conflicting values cannot both gather
//     a quorum, because their quorums would have to share f+1 replicas — more than
//     the fault budget — forcing an honest replica to have voted for both, which an
//     honest replica never does. No split-brain. Ever.
fn two_quorums_overlap(n: u32, f: u32, q: u32) -> u32
    requires(f >= 0) requires(f < 1000000)
    requires(n == 3 * f + 1)
    requires(q == 2 * f + 1)
    ensures(result >= f + 1)
{
    return q + q - n;
}

// (3) Every two-quorum overlap contains at least one HONEST replica. The overlap
//     is f+1 replicas and at most f are faulty, so >= 1 is honest. That honest
//     replica is the witness that makes conflicting commits impossible.
fn overlap_has_honest(f: u32, overlap: u32) -> u32
    requires(f >= 0) requires(f < 1000000)
    requires(overlap == f + 1)
    ensures(result >= 1)
{
    return overlap - f;
}

// (4) View numbers advance monotonically — a new leadership epoch never reuses an
//     old view number, so stale proposals from a deposed leader can never be
//     mistaken for the current round.
fn view_advances(v: u32) -> u32
    requires(v >= 0) requires(v < 1000000)
    ensures(result > v)
{
    return v + 1;
}

// (5) Log positions (sequence numbers) strictly increase — an honest replica never
//     rebinds a slot it has already committed. This is the structural reason an
//     equivocator cannot overwrite committed history.
fn seq_advances(s: u32) -> u32
    requires(s >= 0) requires(s < 1000000)
    ensures(result > s)
{
    return s + 1;
}

// (6) A vote tally can never exceed the size of the electorate. Proved via a loop
//     invariant discharged by the Hoare rule (holds on entry; preserved by one
//     iteration under the loop condition + frame). This is the arithmetic that
//     forbids ballot-stuffing beyond n replicas.
fn tally_bounded(n: u32) -> u32
    requires(n >= 0) requires(n < 1000000)
    ensures(result <= n)
{
    let mut counted = 0;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < n invariant(counted <= i) invariant(i <= n) {
        counted = counted + 1;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return counted;
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 2 — THE PROTOCOL DATA MODEL  (enums, structs, traits)
// =============================================================================

// The three-phase-commit ISA of a PBFT-style protocol.
enum Phase {
    PrePrepare,   // the leader proposes a value for (view, seq)
    Prepare,      // a replica certifies it saw exactly one proposal
    Commit,       // a replica certifies a quorum prepared the value
}

// The fault model. Honest replicas follow the protocol; Silent ones have crashed
// (or are partitioned); an Equivocator is Set — it double-proposes as leader and
// double-votes as a follower, trying to manufacture a split-brain.
enum Role {
    Honest,
    Silent,
    Equivocator,
}

// A protocol message on the wire.
struct Msg {
    phase: Phase,
    view: int,
    seq: int,
    value: int,
    from: int,
}

// A replica (a "seat" on the tribunal).
struct Seat {
    id: int,
    role: Role,
    log: [int],     // its committed history (one value per decided seq)
}

// What the council decides for a sequence slot — the auditor-facing verdict.
enum Verdict {
    Committed { value: int, seq: int, view: int },   // agreement reached
    NoQuorum { seq: int },                           // safe stall: no false commit
}

// A trait with a default method, implemented by the verdict enum: render an
// operator-readable line built entirely from the required accessor.
trait Report {
    fn line(self);                                   // required
    fn banner(self) { "  ▸ ${self.line()}" }         // default, built from the interface
}
impl Report for Verdict {
    fn line(self) {
        match self {
            Verdict::Committed { value: v, seq: s, view: w } =>
                "seq ${s}: COMMIT value=${v} (settled in view ${w})",
            Verdict::NoQuorum { seq: s } =>
                "seq ${s}: NO-QUORUM — safe stall, nothing committed",
        }
    }
}

// Methods on the fault model, so the engine reads in domain language.
impl Seat {
    fn is_silent(self) { match self.role { Role::Silent => true, _ => false } }
    fn is_equivocator(self) { match self.role { Role::Equivocator => true, _ => false } }
    fn is_honest(self) { match self.role { Role::Honest => true, _ => false } }
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 3 — HASHING & SEALS  (tamper-evident ledger primitives)
// =============================================================================

// FNV-1a over a string — the same self-sealing hash used across the Anubis corpus.
fn fnv_str(s) {
    let mut h = 2166136261;
    for i in 0..len(s) {
        h = (h * 16777619 + ord(char_at(s, i))) % 2147483647;
    }
    return h;
}

// Chain a new committed value onto a running ledger hash (hash-chained SMR log).
fn chain_append(prev, value, seq) {
    return fnv_str("${prev}|${seq}:${value}");
}

// Seal any list of values into one integrity tag.
fn seal(entries) {
    let mut h = 2166136261;
    for e in entries {
        h = (h * 16777619 + fnv_str(str(e))) % 2147483647;
    }
    return h;
}

// A digest binding a proposal to its (view, seq) — an equivocation shows up as two
// different digests carrying the same (view, seq) from the same leader.
fn digest(value, view, seq) {
    return fnv_str("v${view}#${seq}=${value}");
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 4 — QUORUM ARITHMETIC & VOTE TALLYING  (runtime side of Part 1)
// =============================================================================

// The runtime quorum size. Its safety (2f+1 > f, and two quorums intersect in an
// honest replica) is exactly what Part 1 proves at check-time.
fn quorum(f) { return 2 * f + 1; }

// Round-robin leader for a view.
fn leader_of(view, n) { return view % n; }

// Generic: count the elements of a list that satisfy a predicate. Type parameter is
// erased (values are dynamic) but documents intent — a reusable quorum counter.
fn count_where<T>(xs, pred) -> u32 {
    return count(xs, pred);
}

// Dedupe votes to at most one per (from, phase): an honest replica votes once per
// phase per round. A second vote from the same seat in the same phase is an
// equivocation — we keep the first and report the rejected doubles. This is the
// runtime enforcement of Part 1's "log positions strictly increase" (seq_advances).
fn dedup_votes(msgs) {
    let mut kept = [];
    let mut seen = {};
    let mut rejected = 0;
    for m in msgs {
        let k = "${m.from}:${phase_tag(m.phase)}";
        if has_key(seen, k) {
            rejected = rejected + 1;      // double vote from the same seat → rejected
        } else {
            seen[k] = 1;
            push(kept, m);
        }
    }
    return (kept, rejected);
}

// A small integer tag per phase (used for tally keys and dedup keys).
fn phase_tag(p) {
    match p {
        Phase::PrePrepare => 0,
        Phase::Prepare    => 1,
        Phase::Commit     => 2,
    }
}

// Tally a bag of messages by their proposed value → { "value": count }.
fn tally_by_value(msgs) {
    let mut t = {};
    for m in msgs {
        let k = str(m.value);
        t[k] = get(t, k, 0) + 1;
    }
    return t;
}

// Return Some(value) for the first value whose tally reaches the quorum, else None.
// (Two-quorums-overlap guarantees at most one value can qualify — see Part 1.)
fn value_with_quorum(tally, q) {
    for k in tally {
        if tally[k] >= q { return Some(int(k)); }
    }
    return None;
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 5 — THE PROTOCOL ENGINE  (three-phase agreement over one sequence slot)
// =============================================================================

// The leader proposes. An honest (or, in this slot, non-silent) leader emits ONE
// PrePrepare. Set-as-leader (Equivocator) emits TWO conflicting PrePreprares — the
// classic equivocation. A Silent leader emits none (it has crashed / timed out).
fn leader_propose(seats, leader, view, seq, base_value) {
    let ldr = seats[leader];
    let mut out = [];
    if ldr.is_silent() {
        return out;                              // crash → no proposal → timeout
    }
    push(out, Msg { phase: Phase::PrePrepare, view: view, seq: seq, value: base_value, from: leader });
    if ldr.is_equivocator() {
        // Set proposes a SECOND, conflicting value for the very same (view, seq).
        push(out, Msg { phase: Phase::PrePrepare, view: view, seq: seq, value: base_value + 999, from: leader });
    }
    return out;
}

// Prepare phase. Each non-silent honest replica that sees EXACTLY ONE proposal for
// (view, seq) certifies it with a Prepare. If it sees conflicting proposals it
// refuses and raises a view-change request — it will not certify an equivocation.
// An Equivocator follower double-votes (two Prepares) to try to inflate a tally;
// dedup_votes strips the extra one.
fn cast_prepares(seats, preprepares, view, seq) {
    let values = unique(map(preprepares, |m| m.value));
    let mut prepares = [];
    let mut viewchange = 0;
    for s in seats {
        if s.is_silent() { continue; }
        if len(values) == 0 { continue; }
        if len(values) > 1 {
            viewchange = viewchange + 1;         // saw an equivocation → demand a new leader
            continue;
        }
        let v = values[0];
        push(prepares, Msg { phase: Phase::Prepare, view: view, seq: seq, value: v, from: s.id });
        if s.is_equivocator() {
            // Set-as-follower casts a conflicting second Prepare.
            push(prepares, Msg { phase: Phase::Prepare, view: view, seq: seq, value: v + 999, from: s.id });
        }
    }
    return (prepares, viewchange);
}

// Commit phase. Every non-silent honest replica that saw the value reach a prepare
// quorum casts a Commit for it. Equivocators again try to double-vote.
fn cast_commits(seats, value, view, seq) {
    let mut commits = [];
    for s in seats {
        if s.is_silent() { continue; }
        push(commits, Msg { phase: Phase::Commit, view: view, seq: seq, value: value, from: s.id });
        if s.is_equivocator() {
            push(commits, Msg { phase: Phase::Commit, view: view, seq: seq, value: value + 999, from: s.id });
        }
    }
    return commits;
}

// Drive one sequence slot to a decision, changing views (leaders) as needed until a
// quorum commits or the view budget is exhausted. Returns (Verdict, telemetry).
//
// The whole safety story lives here: an equivocating or crashed leader produces no
// prepare quorum, which forces a view change to the next (honest) leader; a value
// only commits once a genuine 2f+1 quorum both prepares AND commits it; and if too
// many replicas are faulty for any quorum to form, the slot returns NoQuorum — a
// safe stall, never a false or split commit.
fn agree(seats, seq, base_value, n, f, max_views) {
    let q = quorum(f);
    let mut view = 0;
    let mut prepare_rejects = 0;
    let mut commit_rejects = 0;
    let mut equivocations = 0;

    while view < max_views {
        let leader = leader_of(view, n);
        let preprepares = leader_propose(seats, leader, view, seq, base_value);

        if len(preprepares) == 0 {
            view = view + 1;                     // silent leader → timeout → next view
            continue;
        }
        if len(preprepares) > 1 {
            equivocations = equivocations + 1;   // caught the leader double-proposing
        }

        // --- Prepare phase (dedup, then tally, then test for a quorum) ---
        let cast_p = cast_prepares(seats, preprepares, view, seq);
        let raw_prepares = cast_p[0];
        let viewchange = cast_p[1];
        let dp = dedup_votes(raw_prepares);
        let prepares = dp[0];
        prepare_rejects = prepare_rejects + dp[1];

        let prepared = value_with_quorum(tally_by_value(prepares), q);

        match prepared {
            None => { view = view + 1; }         // no prepare quorum → change view
            Some(v) => {
                // --- Commit phase ---
                let cast_c = cast_commits(seats, v, view, seq);
                let dc = dedup_votes(cast_c);
                let commits = dc[0];
                commit_rejects = commit_rejects + dc[1];

                match value_with_quorum(tally_by_value(commits), q) {
                    Some(cv) => {
                        let tele = { "prepare_rejects": prepare_rejects, "commit_rejects": commit_rejects, "equivocations": equivocations, "views_used": view + 1 };
                        return (Verdict::Committed { value: cv, seq: seq, view: view }, tele);
                    }
                    None => { view = view + 1; }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    let tele = { "prepare_rejects": prepare_rejects, "commit_rejects": commit_rejects, "equivocations": equivocations, "views_used": max_views };
    return (Verdict::NoQuorum { seq: seq }, tele);
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 6 — AGREEMENT: APPLY A VERDICT TO EVERY HONEST REPLICA'S LOG
// =============================================================================
// Values pass by value across the call boundary, so we return a fresh seat list
// (functional update) rather than mutating in place. Each honest replica appends a
// committed value to its own log; silent/faulty replicas are left as-is.
fn apply_verdict(seats, verdict) {
    let mut out = [];
    for s in seats {
        let mut lg = s.log;
        match verdict {
            Verdict::Committed { value: v, seq: _sq, view: _w } => {
                if s.is_honest() { push(lg, v); }
            }
            Verdict::NoQuorum { seq: _sq } => { }
        }
        push(out, Seat { id: s.id, role: s.role, log: lg });
    }
    return out;
}

// Agreement check: every honest replica must hold an identical committed log.
fn honest_logs_agree(seats) {
    let honest = filter(seats, |s| s.is_honest());
    if len(honest) == 0 { return true; }
    let reference = honest[0].log;
    return all(honest, |s| s.log == reference);
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 7 — COUNCIL CONSTRUCTION
// =============================================================================
// Build a council of n = 3f+1 seats, marking specific ids as Silent or Equivocator.
fn make_council(n, silent_ids, equiv_ids) {
    let mut seats = [];
    for i in 0..n {
        let role = if contains(equiv_ids, i) {
            Role::Equivocator
        } else if contains(silent_ids, i) {
            Role::Silent
        } else {
            Role::Honest
        };
        push(seats, Seat { id: i, role: role, log: [] });
    }
    return seats;
}

fn role_name(s) {
    match s.role {
        Role::Honest => "honest",
        Role::Silent => "silent",
        Role::Equivocator => "SET/equivocator",
    }
}

fn describe_council(seats) {
    let names = map(seats, |s| "#${s.id}:${role_name(s)}");
    return join(names, "  ");
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 8 — SCENARIO RUNNER
// =============================================================================
// Runs one labelled scenario end-to-end: builds the council, drives agreement over
// one sequence slot, applies the verdict to honest logs, checks Agreement, and
// prints an operator line. Returns a compact result record used for the final seal.
fn run_scenario(label, n, f, base_value, silent_ids, equiv_ids, expect_commit) {
    let seats = make_council(n, silent_ids, equiv_ids);
    print("");
    print("── ${label}");
    print("   council(n=${n}, f=${f}): ${describe_council(seats)}");

    let outcome = agree(seats, 1, base_value, n, f, n + 1);
    let verdict = outcome[0];
    let tele = outcome[1];

    let after = apply_verdict(seats, verdict);
    let agree_ok = honest_logs_agree(after);

    print("   ${verdict.banner()}");
    print("   quorum=${quorum(f)}  views_used=${tele["views_used"]}  equivocations_caught=${tele["equivocations"]}  double_votes_rejected=${tele["prepare_rejects"] + tele["commit_rejects"]}");

    // Determine committed value (0 sentinel if none) and whether the run met its
    // stated expectation — a commit scenario must commit; a stall scenario must not.
    let committed = match verdict {
        Verdict::Committed { value: v, seq: _s, view: _w } => v,
        Verdict::NoQuorum { seq: _s } => 0,
    };
    let did_commit = match verdict {
        Verdict::Committed { value: _v, seq: _s, view: _w } => true,
        Verdict::NoQuorum { seq: _s } => false,
    };

    let honest_agreement = agree_ok;
    let meets_expectation = did_commit == expect_commit;
    let ok = honest_agreement && meets_expectation;

    print("   Agreement (all honest logs identical): ${honest_agreement}   expectation(${if expect_commit { "commit" } else { "stall" }}): ${meets_expectation}   → ${if ok { "PASS" } else { "FAIL" }}");

    return { "label": label, "committed": committed, "did_commit": did_commit, "agreement": honest_agreement, "ok": ok };
}


// =============================================================================
// PART 9 — MAIN: DRIVE THE TRIBUNAL THROUGH FIVE SCENARIOS
// =============================================================================
fn main() {
    print("═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");
    print("  ENNEAD — a Byzantine-fault-tolerant consensus kernel in Anubis");
    print("═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");

    // The safety envelope. At `anubis check` these carry Z3 proofs; here they simply
    // compute — the same functions, two readings.
    print("");
    print("proved safety kernel (each discharged by Z3 at check-time):");
    print("  quorum_exceeds_faults(1) = ${quorum_exceeds_faults(1)}     |= result > f            (a quorum is never all-faulty)");
    print("  two_quorums_overlap(4,1,3) = ${two_quorums_overlap(4, 1, 3)}   |= result >= f+1         (quorum-intersection lemma)");
    print("  overlap_has_honest(1,2) = ${overlap_has_honest(1, 2)}      |= result >= 1           (an honest witness in every overlap)");
    print("  view_advances(7) = ${view_advances(7)}             |= result > v            (no view-number reuse)");
    print("  seq_advances(41) = ${seq_advances(41)}            |= result > s            (log slots strictly advance)");
    print("  tally_bounded(5) = ${tally_bounded(5)}             |= result <= n           (no ballot-stuffing; loop-invariant)");

    print("");
    print("live council (three-phase agreement; f Byzantine faults tolerated at n=3f+1):");

    let mut results = [];

    // Scenario 1 — happy path. n=4, f=1, everyone honest. One view, clean commit.
    push(results, run_scenario(
        "Scenario 1: quorum of honest judges (n=4, f=1)",
        4, 1, 100, [], [], true));

    // Scenario 2 — the leader has crashed. View 0's leader is silent; the council
    // times out and the view-1 leader carries the round to a commit.
    push(results, run_scenario(
        "Scenario 2: crashed leader → view change (n=4, f=1, seat #0 silent)",
        4, 1, 200, [0], [], true));

    // Scenario 3 — SET. The view-0 leader (seat #0) equivocates, double-proposing
    // two conflicting values, and also double-votes as a follower. Honest replicas
    // refuse to certify the conflict, force a view change, and the honest view-1
    // leader commits the ONE agreed value. No split-brain; the double-votes are
    // rejected. This is the quorum-intersection lemma doing its job at runtime.
    push(results, run_scenario(
        "Scenario 3: THE EQUIVOCATION ATTACK — Set on the throne (n=4, f=1, seat #0 = Set)",
        4, 1, 300, [], [0], true));

    // Scenario 4 — too many faults. With 2 of 4 seats silent (exceeding f=1) no
    // quorum of 2f+1=3 can ever form. The council must STALL safely — return
    // NoQuorum — rather than commit a value only a minority saw. Fail-closed.
    push(results, run_scenario(
        "Scenario 4: beyond the fault budget → safe stall (n=4, f=1, seats #0,#2 silent)",
        4, 1, 400, [0, 2], [], false));

    // Scenario 5 — a full Ennead. n=7 (f=2) with one equivocator AND one crash. The
    // council still musters a 2f+1=5 quorum among the honest and commits; agreement
    // holds across all honest logs despite compound Byzantine + crash faults.
    push(results, run_scenario(
        "Scenario 5: the nine-seat tribunal under compound faults (n=7, f=2, #1=Set, #4 silent)",
        7, 2, 700, [4], [1], true));

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Cross-scenario Agreement + tamper-evident seal of the committed history.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    print("");
    print("─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────");
    print("integrity roll-up:");

    let committed_values = map(filter(results, |r| r["did_commit"]), |r| r["committed"]);
    print("  committed values (in scenario order) = ${committed_values}");

    // Hash-chain the committed history into a tamper-evident SMR ledger.
    let mut chain = 2166136261;
    let mut positions = [];
    let mut seq = 1;
    for v in committed_values {
        chain = chain_append(chain, v, seq);
        push(positions, "seq${seq}=${v}→${chain}");
        seq = seq_advances(seq);          // the proved monotone advance, used for real
    }
    for p in positions { print("    ${p}"); }

    // Global verdict: every scenario met its expectation AND kept Agreement.
    let all_ok = all(results, |r| r["ok"]);
    let all_agree = all(results, |r| r["agreement"]);
    let verdicts = map(results, |r| if r["ok"] { 1 } else { 0 });

    print("");
    print("  scenarios: ${len(results)}   all-agreement=${all_agree}   all-expected=${all_ok}");
    print("  ledger head = ${chain}");
    print("  SEAL#${seal(committed_values)}  verdict=${if all_ok && all_agree { "PASS" } else { "FAIL" }}");
    print("═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");

    // A single integrity tag over every scenario verdict — the artifact's identity.
    return seal(verdicts);
}
